@startuml
title Region solve - full pipeline (current, plain-language)

start

partition "before ANY world gen (build time, once)" {
  :for every possible group of up to 8 biomes,\ncheck at least one biome in the list is legal\nnext to all of them at the same time;
  if (any group has NO such biome?) then (yes)
    :CRASH the build on purpose -\nthe biome list itself has a real gap,\nfix it before shipping;
    end
  else (no)
  endif
  note right
    this is what makes the final "CRASH on purpose" box
    far below PROVEN unreachable, not just rare - a real
    production bug (badlands + ocean shared no third
    common neighbor) was found and fixed this way.
  end note
}

:place seed points across the region\n(SeedGrid, +margin so edge triangles have real neighbors);
:connect seed points into triangles\n(Delaunay triangulation);
:work out which triangles touch which\n(TriangleAdjacency, max 3 real neighbors each);

partition "pick each triangle's biome + size (CSP)" {
  :any triangle whose center already falls inside\na neighboring region's solved area gets FIXED\nto that neighbor's own decision;
  :work out a sensible max triangle count per biome\n(how many of ITS OWN patches could fit in ITS\nfair share of this region's area - same number\nfor every biome once you're fair by area);
  :for every other (free) triangle, pick a biome that is\nallowed next to all its already-decided neighbors,\nand give it a target size from that biome's own range;
  note right
    picks are ALSO fairness-biased: each biome has a
    target share of triangles (based on its size range,
    squared, since a patch is an area not a line) - an
    under-represented biome is preferred whenever two
    choices are equally legal, UNLESS that biome already
    hit its max triangle count for this region (then it
    sorts last, but can still be picked if truly the only
    legal option - never blocks a solution from existing).
  end note
  :backtrack if a choice leads to a dead end;
}

partition "grow each triangle's real shape, cell by cell\n(SeamGrowthRasterizer)" {
  :turn each triangle's target size into\na target CELL COUNT;
  note right
    from here on, the triangle mesh is only ever
    consulted for two things: each patch's target
    size, and roughly where it starts. A biome's
    actual pixel SHAPE is never decided by measuring
    against raw triangle positions - it's grown
    outward from whatever its already-decided
    neighbors leave behind (this region's own
    growing patches, AND already-solved neighbor
    regions), one legality-checked cell at a time.
    This is what closes the old "thin bridge biome"
    problem at the root instead of patching it after
    the fact.
  end note

  partition "head start - before any real competition" {
    :for each triangle, in a fixed, repeatable order,\nclaim its own seed cell;
    :try to claim a few more cells right next to it -\nup to its biome's own "stay together" minimum -\nusing the SAME legal / stay-together / no-pinch\nrules as the main growth step below;
    note right
      without this, every triangle's first cell sits
      at the exact same top priority, so who gets a
      SECOND cell is pure luck of processing order.
      Measured directly on a real export: 95% of the
      resulting 1-cell specks were boxed in by other,
      perfectly LEGAL neighbors before they ever got a
      chance to grow. A small head start fixes that
      before real competition starts.
    end note
  }

  partition "main growth - one shared queue for the whole region" {
    :put every triangle's own next candidate cell into\nONE shared queue, sorted by\n"most behind its own target size" first,\nthen by distance to its own seed;
    while (queue not empty?) is (yes)
      :take the top candidate cell;
      if (already claimed by someone else?) then (yes)
        :skip it - stale entry;
      else (no)
        if (legal against every already-decided\nneighbor around it, all 8 directions?) then (no)
          :skip it;
        else (yes)
        endif
        if (this triangle's patch is past its own\n"stay together" minimum size, AND this cell\nwouldn't have enough same-biome neighbors?) then (yes)
          :skip it - would break up its own shape;
        else (no)
        endif
        if (claiming it would turn an already-decided\nneighbor into a 1-cell-wide sliver?) then (yes)
          :skip it - soft reject, another candidate\nor the gap-fill step still covers it;
        else (no)
          :claim the cell for this triangle;
          :offer its neighbor cells to whoever can\nlegally use them next - THIS triangle itself\n(guaranteed continuation), AND the\nbest-placed other triangle of any other\nlegal biome;
        endif
      endif
    endwhile (empty)
  }

  partition "fill any leftover gaps" {
    :repeat until nothing changes:\nfor any still-unclaimed cell, give it whichever\nbiome matches the most already-decided\nneighbors and stays legal;
    :a handful of cells have NO decided neighbor at\nall yet (a rare mesh-warp gap) -\ngive those to the nearest triangle's biome instead;
    if (still stuck after repeating until\nnothing changes?) then (yes)
      :CRASH on purpose - the build-time check above\nalready proves this can't happen for a\nwell-formed biome list, so reaching this\nmeans that proof was skipped or the list\nchanged since;
      end
    else (no)
    endif
  }
}

partition "check the result before shipping it (every run)" {
  :scan the whole finished region for illegal\ntouches (straight AND diagonal), tiny leftover\nspecks, and 1-cell-wide slivers;
  note right
    a diagonal same-biome neighbor rescues a cell
    from being counted as a sliver or a speck - a
    real, thicker diagonal shape shouldn't be
    miscounted just because this check only looked
    at straight up/down/left/right.
  end note
  if (any illegal touch found?) then (yes)
    :this should never happen - print it loud\nand fail the run;
  else (no)
  endif
  if (any speck or sliver found?) then (yes)
    :cosmetic only, not a crash -\nstill print it loud so it never gets ignored;
  else (no)
  endif
}

:done - one biome per cell, zero illegal adjacencies,\nshape grown from real neighbors the whole way;

stop

@enduml
